{"timestamp":"2026-10-28T14:33:48.238Z","ordinal":460102,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a181ab-bb8d-7471-bcfe-7451f754e731","turn_id":"01a182ec-dd91-76f3-bef9-ba00d38b5f54","session_id":"01a181ab-bb8d-7471-bcfe-7451f754e731","root_turn_id":"01a182ec-dd91-76f3-bef9-ba00d38b5f54","response_id":"resp_0db389702a7164cc016ae207cad920819f98d9b1fc065d870c","usage":{"input_tokens":65539,"cached_input_tokens":65152,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":119,"reasoning_output_tokens":29,"total_tokens":65658},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":2300529,"cached_input_tokens":2226176,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":7667,"reasoning_output_tokens":3371,"total_tokens":2308196},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":8527178752,"cached_input_tokens":8435958272,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":24338436,"reasoning_output_tokens":14080236,"total_tokens":8551517188}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-28T14:33:49.720Z","ordinal":460103,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a181ab-bb8d-7471-bcfe-7451f754e731","turn_id":"01a182ec-dd91-76f3-bef9-ba00d38b5f54","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-88e123c6-9871-48fd-aa9e-eecd4ab2861b","process_id":"11894","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","cat /home/mike/.agents/skills/using-entire/SKILL.md; rg -n \"Nexus|Entire|RC10|PR 302|release\" /home/mike/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-code","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"cat /home/mike/.agents/skills/using-entire/SKILL.md","name":"SKILL.md","path":"/home/mike/.agents/skills/using-entire/SKILL.md"},{"type":"search","cmd":"rg -n 'Nexus|Entire|RC10|PR 302|release' /home/mike/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md","query":"Nexus|Entire|RC10|PR 302|release","path":"MEMORY.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"---\nname: using-entire\ndescription: >\n  Use as the FIRST action on any research, investigation, or\n  codebase-understanding request — \"research X\", \"investigate X\", \"look into\n  X\", \"dig into X\", \"search X\", \"explore X\", \"understand X\", \"why is this like\n  this\" — before Glob, Grep, or Read. Recorded intent lives in Entire\n  checkpoints on the server, not in local files, so an empty or unfamiliar\n  working copy is not a reason to skip it. Routes to the right Entire sub-skill\n  (search, recall, explain, what-happened, replay, teach, review,\n  session-handoff).\n---\n\n# Using Entire\n\nYour default when exploring or understanding code should be: **read the\nrecorded intent, don't guess.**\n\nEntire checkpoints capture the prompts, transcripts, and decisions behind every\nagent-driven change. When you need to understand why code exists or how a module\nevolved, look up the checkpoint history first. Only fall back to inference from\ncode structure when no history is available — and label that explicitly.\n\n## When This Skill Activates\n\nUse this skill whenever the task involves:\n\n- A request phrased as \"research X\", \"investigate X\", \"look into X\", \"dig into\n  X\", or \"find out how X works\"\n- Exploring or understanding an unfamiliar module or file\n- Preparing to refactor, extend, or debug code you didn't write\n- Answering \"why is this like this?\" or \"what was the intent?\"\n- Doing pre-work research before making changes\n- Any codebase exploration where historical context would help\n\nFor a research- or investigate-shaped request, the very first action is an\n`entire search` via the `search` skill. Do not open source files or grep first;\nthe recorded history says what was tried and why, and code reads come after to\nconfirm it.\n\nDo **not** use this skill for simple, well-understood edits where you already\nhave full context (e.g. \"add a comment to line 5\").\n\n## First Step: Check Repository Status\n\nBefore diving into specific lookups, check whether Entire is available and\nthe repository has checkpoint history:\n\n```bash\nentire status\n```\n\nThis tells you whether Entire is enabled, which git hooks are installed, whether\nthe metadata branch is initialized, and any active sessions on the current\nbranch.\n\n## Scenario Routing\n\nWhen this skill activates, determine which sub-skill best fits the user's need:\n\n| Scenario | Typical user expressions | Delegate to |\n|----------|------------------------|-------------|\n| Code block provenance | \"why is this like this\" / \"wtf is going on\" / \"what happened at src/auth.ts:42\" / \"tell me why this changed\" | `what-happened` |\n| Research or investigate a topic | \"research X\" / \"investigate X\" / \"look into X\" / \"dig into X\" / \"what do we know about X\" | `search` first, then this skill's exploration flow if needed |\n| Find prior work | \"has anyone done X before\" / \"search past work for rate limiting\" / \"find the previous implementation\" | `search` |\n| Recall a task playbook | \"have we done this before\" / \"recall how we did X\" / \"how did we do this last time\" | `recall` |\n| Replay feature history | \"replay <feature>\" / \"walk me through how X was built\" / \"show me the journey of Y\" | `replay` |\n| Teach a repo topic | \"teach me <topic>\" / \"how does <topic> work in this repo\" / \"give me a lesson on X\" | `teach` |\n| Understand original intent | \"explain this function\" / \"explain parseConfig\" / \"what was the intent behind src/auth.ts\" | `explain` |\n| Generate a dispatch / summary | \"summarize recent work\" / \"generate a dispatch\" / \"what was accomplished this week\" | run `entire dispatch` directly |\n| Code review with intent context | \"review these changes\" / \"review this branch\" / \"audit before merging\" | `review` |\n| Hand off to another agent | \"hand off this session\" / \"continue in another agent\" / \"pick up the codex session\" | `session-handoff` |\n| Convert session to skill | \"turn this into a skill\" / \"make a skill from this session\" | `session-to-skill` |\n| Link session to other repos | \"link session to other repos\" / \"attach session to the foo repo\" | `session-crosslink` |\n| General exploration | \"understand this module\" / \"explore how auth works\" / \"help me understand the codebase\" | _(self — see below)_ |\n\nIf the scenario clearly maps to a sub-skill, delegate entirely to that skill's\nworkflow. Do not duplicate their steps here.\n\n## General Exploration Flow\n\nWhen no specific sub-skill fits — e.g. the user asks to \"understand this\nmodule\" or \"explore how auth works\" — use this flow:\n\n1. **Identify key files** in the target area (entry points, main types, core\n   functions).\n\n2. **Check for checkpoint coverage** on each key file:\n\n```bash\ngit log --format='%H %s' -5 -- <file>\n```\n\nLook for `Entire-Checkpoint:` trailers in the commit bodies:\n\n```bash\ngit log --format='%H %b' -5 -- <file> | grep -B1 'Entire-Checkpoint:'\n```\n\n3. **Read intent for covered commits** — extract the checkpoint ID from the\n   trailer, then use JSON output for non-interactive consumption:\n\n```bash\nentire checkpoint explain --checkpoint <checkpoint-id> --json --no-pager\n```\n\nIf you only have a commit hash (not a checkpoint ID), use:\n\n```bash\nentire checkpoint explain --commit <sha> --no-pager\n```\n\n4. **Synthesize**: combine the recorded intent (from checkpoints) with the\n   current code structure to build a comprehensive understanding.\n\n5. **Report** your findings, clearly distinguishing:\n   - Facts backed by checkpoint transcripts (label as \"recorded intent\")\n   - Observations inferred from code alone (label as \"inferred from code\")\n\n## AI Agent Usage Notes\n\nWhen running Entire commands from an agent subprocess:\n\n- **Always pass `--json`** for commands that support it and might prompt\n  interactively. `--json` implies non-interactive output.\n- **Always pass `--no-pager`** to prevent pager activation in subprocess\n  contexts.\n- **Always specify `--checkpoint <id>` or `--commit <sha>`** for\n  `entire checkpoint explain` — without a locator, the command falls back to an\n  interactive picker.\n\n## When No Checkpoints Exist\n\nIf the target code predates Entire or has no checkpoint coverage:\n\n1. Say explicitly: \"No Entire checkpoint history is available for this code.\"\n2. Proceed with standard code understanding (read source, check git log, trace\n   call sites).\n3. Label all explanations as \"inferred from code — not backed by recorded\n   intent.\"\n4. If the user wants deeper history, suggest `search` with related keywords —\n   there may be checkpoints on related work even if the specific file isn't\n   directly covered.\n\n## Transparency Principle\n\nAlways make it clear whether your answer comes from:\n\n- **Checkpoint-backed history** — you read the actual session transcript and\n  know the developer's stated intent\n- **Code inference** — you analyzed the code structure and are reasoning about\n  probable intent\n\nNever present inferred intent as if it were recorded fact. The core value of\nthis workflow is that distinction.\n\n## References\n\nFor a deeper understanding of Entire's capabilities beyond what the sub-skills\ncover, consult these resources:\n\n- **CLI help**: run `entire help` to see all available commands and flags\n- **Documentation**: https://docs.entire.io/llms.txt — AI-friendly\n  documentation index following the [llms.txt standard](https://llmstxt.org),\n  designed to be consumed directly by LLMs and coding agents\n1:# Task Group: Nexus Code client release and Cloudflare contest publication\n2:scope: Package the installed Nexus Code Linux client and prepare the distinct Nexus Workers/Artifacts contest submission in `/home/mike/nexus-code`; retain separate readiness dispositions and do not treat CI or offline fixtures as a clean-account proof.\n3:applies_to: cwd=/home/mike/nexus-code; reuse_rule=recheck the exact release revision, downloadable artifact, endpoint configuration, contest rules, Cloudflare account, broker installation, and credentials; this records a 2026-10-23 partial result, not portable release or contest readiness.\n9:- rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md (cwd=/home/mike/nexus-code, rollout_path=/home/mike/.codex/sessions/2026/10/13/rollout-2026-10-13T16-47-30-01a13629-7b99-7b61-a6e3-88bb4ac1dd15.jsonl, updated_at=2026-10-23T04:47:40+00:00, thread_id=01a13629-7b99-7b61-a6e3-88bb4ac1dd15, contest CI/video/submission material verified; clean-account reproducibility blocked)\n15:## Task 2: Package Nexus Code as a complete Linux client with installer and CI, partial\n19:- rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md (cwd=/home/mike/nexus-code, rollout_path=/home/mike/.codex/sessions/2026/10/13/rollout-2026-10-13T16-47-30-01a13629-7b99-7b61-a6e3-88bb4ac1dd15.jsonl, updated_at=2026-10-23T04:47:40+00:00, thread_id=01a13629-7b99-7b61-a6e3-88bb4ac1dd15, prerelease/installer evidence exists; representative inference remains unverified)\n27:- for Cloudflare contest work, the user required using the existing repository, preserving unrelated history/work, and distinguishing the Nexus Code client from the Cloudflare Workers/Artifacts submission -> do not replace repositories or wholesale-integrate unaccepted candidates. [Task 1]\n30:- the user required exact downloadable-artifact and clean-environment verification before declaring readiness -> packaging structure, a release workflow, or a fresh-home install alone is insufficient. [Task 2]\n34:- Contest-specific CI passed at `ce7e58b9ce3f6e4a72f84d205c5319b241ca0aa9` in workflow `38471712649`; it covered demo tests and Worker build/typecheck, not a real Cloudflare account run. The service is the submission; Nexus Code is its client/demo harness. [Task 1]\n37:- The stated support envelope is Linux x86-64/glibc with Node.js 20+; do not claim portability beyond it. The rollout records prerelease `v0.1.0-alpha.1`, release workflow `38459471967`, checksum `39548ab8b6ba4043e930f8a30d99ce6f9f787f68c8c46b1b10a9a2f1ea7e5ec8`, and fresh-home installer verification. [Task 2]\n45:# Task Group: Nexus agent effectiveness: unbounded continuation and evidence-bounded diagnosis\n46:scope: Review failed Nexus agent exports and maintain prompt/regression safeguards for progressing continuation loops, direct diagnostic questions, and timeout claims in `/home/mike/nexus-code`.\n57:- Nexus, nexus-session-session_20261014_091558_9m78.json, CONTEXT_CAPACITY_EXCEEDED, 54582+8192+4096>65536, make test, timeout, truncated output, direct status question, evidence-bounded diagnosis\n88:applies_to: cwd=/home/mike/Projects/aihost; reuse_rule=revalidate model/release/inventory/live gateway state and preserve the shared worktree before reuse; the selections and validation results are release-specific, and 22 generic validator checks plus listed vLLM/cancellation limits remain unproven.\n108:- Flash-Next Q-TOOLS passed 7/7 synthetic categories and 20/20 Nexus replay points. Q-LONGCTX passed probes from 16K through 59,913 tokens; a 67,335-token input was rejected with HTTP 400 in 0.14s. Both selected models passed production qualification after reboot and rollback restoration. [Task 1]\n120:applies_to: cwd=/home/mike/Projects/aihost; reuse_rule=revalidate current vllm-top/gateway health contract, polling cadence, release, and host before reuse; this records a successful `v0.10.2` deployment, not a general guarantee for unrelated inventory sources.\n140:- Regression coverage sends stopped then healthy snapshots to the same `App` instance in `app::gateway_lifecycle_tests::gateway_worker_stop_and_recovery_are_reconciled_without_restarting_app`. `cargo test --manifest-path vllm-top/Cargo.toml` passed `141` tests; release build and console-only Ansible deployment passed, with live `vllm-top 0.10.2`. [Task 1]\n146:# Task Group: Nexus dogfood-blockers independent candidate review and compaction evidence integrity\n147:scope: Review-only, isolated validation of `fix/dogfood-blockers` candidates against Nexus baseline; use before selecting an I1/objective-supervisor base or relying on context admission, unseen-result, or modified-file evidence.\n158:- Nexus, fix/dogfood-blockers, 6b34f5f, 2860228, 6f71043, 6845415, unseen tool results, CONTEXT_CAPACITY_EXCEEDED, compaction, modified files, repeated tool-call IDs, editedPaths, editChangedFile, EALLOWSCRIPTS\n168:- Nexus, fix/dogfood-blockers, 1b71e27, 2860228, MAX_READ_LINES, StreamRedactor, tester.ts, verifier baseline, BASELINE_FAILURES_ONLY, EALLOWSCRIPTS, native Git metadata\n172:- for review-only candidate work, the user required: “Do not modify Nexus source”, “use a scratch copy”, “If something is wrong, REPORT IT”, and preserve the operator’s dirty tree -> isolate builds/tests, preserve negative results, and report rather than remediate. [Task 1]\n182:- Build isolated debug/release artifacts before classifying suite results. Archive suites can be distorted by missing Git metadata and host npm `EALLOWSCRIPTS`; compare baseline and candidate, then report environmental failures separately rather than calling either a clean verification. [Task 1][Task 2]\n187:- Symptom: initial isolated suites fail before a candidate is fully built. Cause: required debug/release artifacts were absent. Fix: build scratch-copy artifacts first, then classify tests. [Task 1]\n192:# Task Group: Nexus architecture reconnaissance and context-engineering baseline\n193:scope: Strictly read-only mapping of Nexus information/context flow, durable state, admission, and validation; use when the requested deliverable is an architecture investigation rather than remediation or design work.\n196:## Task 1: Capture baseline for the intended A-I Nexus architecture reconnaissance, incomplete\n204:- Nexus, read-only, architecture reconnaissance, context-engineering, CURRENT-NEXUS-CONTEXT-ARCHITECTURE.md, 2860228, linear transcript resend, context admission, deterministic compaction, durable session state, no context router\n208:- for Nexus reconnaissance, the user required “read-only” and “do not modify source, tests, configuration, documentation, dependencies, Git state” -> preserve scope, record exact snapshot SHA plus concurrent-change risk, and do not implement optimization or prove a preferred theory. [Task 1]\n220:# Task Group: Nexus live Ratatui TUI review and redaction/observer isolation\n222:applies_to: cwd=/home/mike/nexus-code (rollout metadata recorded cwd=/home/mike/Projects/aihost); reuse_rule=inspect the current HEAD and preserve any dirty working tree before reviewing; re-run TypeScript, Rust, and PTY coverage before treating this code-review result as release qualification.\n240:- At reviewed HEAD `2860228`, Nexus had moved from a snapshot viewer to a live Ratatui client: multiline composer, approval overlays, slash commands, cancellation, tool-detail/evidence panels, receipt panels, and default TUI launch in a terminal. `--repl` remains the readline fallback. [Task 1]\n241:- Live launch path: `harness.sh` -> `core/target/release/nexus-tui --live` -> TypeScript `cli/dist/sidecar.js` over NDJSON. The evidence protocol has sidecar-generated evidence IDs, `tool.detail`, and receipt requests; `cli/src/evidence.ts` maintains a bounded redacted ledger. [Task 1]\n251:# Task Group: Nexus CRED-003 FlightRecorder and audit-log credential sanitization\n283:# Task Group: Nexus Cloudflare Artifacts capability, agent-native collaboration, and contest evidence\n327:## Task 5: Prepare the Nexus Workers/Artifacts contest submission, partial\n331:- rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md (cwd=/home/mike/nexus-code, rollout_path=/home/mike/.codex/sessions/2026/10/13/rollout-2026-10-13T16-47-30-01a13629-7b99-7b61-a6e3-88bb4ac1dd15.jsonl, updated_at=2026-10-23T04:47:40+00:00, thread_id=01a13629-7b99-7b61-a6e3-88bb4ac1dd15, contest CI/video/submission material verified; clean-account reproducibility blocked)\n342:- for contest preparation, the user required the existing target repository and unrelated work/history to be preserved; distinguish Nexus Code (client/harness) from Nexus (the submitted service), and never present offline fixtures as real Cloudflare evidence. [Task 5]\n349:- Artifacts should own Git persistence, refs, objects, transport, isolation, forks, and repository token handling; Nexus/collaboration logic should own actor/task authority, intent, semantic contention, validation freshness, acceptance, and provenance. Textual overlap is not duplicate intent, clean merge is not semantic correctness, and ancestry is not authorization or acceptance. Prefer experiments with versioned work statements, exact candidate/evidence links, source/confidence/freshness relevance signals, state-change notifications, and explicit dispositions; avoid all-to-all broadcast reasoning. [Task 2]\n352:- Proposed minimum product: a Worker coordinates two isolated external Nexus runners, uses real Artifacts repositories/forks from one baseline, records exact-SHA validation/review, captures operator disposition, and integrates only the accepted candidate. Durable Objects fit serialized per-run coordination; external Nexus processes should perform shell/Git work rather than running the local agent in Workers. First implementation proof: create/import repository, issue a short-lived scoped token, clone/push a commit, then read that exact commit back. [Task 4]\n366:# Task Group: Nexus Phase 2 independent requirements and architecture review\n370:## Task 1: Independently review Nexus Phase 2 requirements, accepted with recorded concerns for Phase 2S\n378:- Nexus, issue-12, Phase-2, Phase-2S, ACCEPT_WITH_RECORDED_CONCERNS_FOR_PHASE_2S, CAP, AUTH, CTX, DATA, UI, EXEC-001, capability-identity, full-access, context-admission, sensitive-data, no-remote\n526:- security/t5820-credential-remediation-sync, 26b6f7393c317a3578d36da7888c8fb03addb136, 198 passed, 49 failed, 959 passed, Gitleaks, make quality, t5820-release-integrity, t5820-v128-terminal-qualification, git status, explicit worktree paths\n590:applies_to: cwd=/home/mike/Projects/aihost; reuse_rule=revalidate service/release/config state and treat all credentials, sessions, fixture paths, and checkout state as time-specific; never use these notes to bypass authentication, expose secrets, directly reach workers, or mutate protected production services.\n677:applies_to: cwd=/home/mike/Projects/aihost; reuse_rule=recheck authoritative contracts, acceptance records, release identities, and workload/horizon coverage before any readiness declaration; do not infer readiness from deployment health or adjacent phases.\n691:- No canonical `AUTONOMOUS_ENGINEERING_READY` contract or composite acceptance route was found. The verified B+ release, healthy gateway/workers, rollback release, and 65/65 Phase 14 artifacts do not establish AES readiness. [Task 1]\n699:scope: `/home/mike/Projects/aihost` host onboarding, Ubuntu Noble/Resolute platform-baseline work, and the approved commissioning sequence for Dell Precision 5820 `ai-5820-01`; baseline implementation is partial and sandbox review remains a release-blocking boundary.\n712:## Task 2: Implement release-aware Noble/Resolute baseline with pre-mutation gates, partial\n733:- The raw bootstrap gate must run before `apt-get update` and accept exactly `ubuntu:24.04:noble` or `ubuntu:26.04:resolute`. Since `ansible.cfg` sets `inject_facts_as_vars = false`, guarded playbooks must use `ansible_facts.distribution`, `ansible_facts.distribution_version`, and `ansible_facts.distribution_release`. [Task 2]\n734:- Release-aware tests must actually render and inspect APT sources. The Noble and Resolute harnesses were digest-pinned, source-rendering-aware, release-pin-aware, and reported `changed=0` on initial and transition idempotency reruns before the final safety finding. [Task 2]\n752:- rollout_summaries/2026-10-16T01-45-07-stRS-t5820_assessment_and_nexus_v1_release_monitoring.md (cwd=/home/mike/Projects/aihost, rollout_path=/home/mike/.codex/sessions/2026/10/16/rollout-2026-10-16T01-45-07-01a14262-6554-7912-9e3b-5f24d58b12c0.jsonl, updated_at=2026-10-18T01:41:41+00:00, thread_id=01a14262-6554-7912-9e3b-5f24d58b12c0, read-only baseline; decision portfolio and five reports unfinished)\n777:# Task Group: Nexus v1 CI prerequisite integration and release qualification handoff\n778:scope: Integrate reviewed CI prerequisites and accurately track Nexus v1 release gates, claims, and held-out boundaries; use for `v1/integration` readiness work, not as a substitute for exact-RC qualification.\n779:applies_to: cwd=/home/mike/nexus-code (worktree used: /home/mike/nexus-wt/p10g-rebased); reuse_rule=recheck integration HEAD, claim ownership/staleness, exact CI, acceptance documents, and release state; recorded alpha.2 status and blockers are time-specific.\n781:## Task 1: Monitor exact-head CI/reviews and merge PR #10 without calling it a release, success for integration; partial for qualification\n785:- rollout_summaries/2026-10-16T01-45-07-stRS-t5820_assessment_and_nexus_v1_release_monitoring.md (cwd=/home/mike/Projects/aihost, rollout_path=/home/mike/.codex/sessions/2026/10/16/rollout-2026-10-16T01-45-07-01a14262-6554-7912-9e3b-5f24d58b12c0.jsonl, updated_at=2026-10-18T01:41:41+00:00, thread_id=01a14262-6554-7912-9e3b-5f24d58b12c0, PR #10 integrated; qualification blocked)\n789:- Nexus v1, PR #10, bdc705e593def11ee5e44a6ebd0703b57e5953a0, CI 39085388798, v1/integration, 2c70d39ad4f9c29cff04c909eef9de6d2a29bdb2, check_requirements.mjs --enforce, REQ-QUAL-101, ACCEPTANCE.md, CONCURRENCY.md\n795:- rollout_summaries/2026-10-16T01-45-07-stRS-t5820_assessment_and_nexus_v1_release_monitoring.md (cwd=/home/mike/Projects/aihost, rollout_path=/home/mike/.codex/sessions/2026/10/16/rollout-2026-10-16T01-45-07-01a14262-6554-7912-9e3b-5f24d58b12c0.jsonl, updated_at=2026-10-18T01:41:41+00:00, thread_id=01a14262-6554-7912-9e3b-5f24d58b12c0, held-out payload preserved; readiness files untouched)\n803:- reviewers and the release workflow distinguish source/CI acceptance from RC/product qualification -> never call a green CI run a release. [Task 1]\n804:- when shared readiness/governance files are claimed, coordinate claim release or wait for the documented stale threshold; do not access held-out payloads or edit the artifact meanwhile. [Task 2]\n809:- At handoff the version remained `0.1.0-alpha.2`; there was no RC, release tag, publication, or qualification run. `node scripts/check_requirements.mjs --enforce` failed 12 rows: `REQ-AGT-002`, `REQ-AGT-010`, `REQ-PRV-003`, `REQ-REL-005`, `REQ-AGT-118`, `REQ-AGT-110`, `REQ-PRV-111`, `REQ-REL-114`, `REQ-QUAL-101`, `REQ-QUAL-107`, `REQ-QUAL-108`, `REQ-QUAL-109`. [Task 1]\n812:- The readiness claims were `FILE_docs_v1.0.0_READINESS.md/owner.json` and `PLAN-10_READINESS_T2/owner.json`; at handoff the owner PID was absent but the claim was younger than 24 hours. Resume after release or the documented stale-claim procedure, update against `2c70d39`, and rerun fresh branch CI. [Task 2]\n817:- Symptom: PR #9 readiness evidence is canceled/skipped or stale. Cause: it was based on the old integration head. Fix: acquire/release the readiness claim, refresh against current integration, then run fresh CI; never infer qualification from its prior checks. [Task 1][Task 2]\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":28566}},"started_at_ms":1793198029719,"completed_at_ms":1793198029720}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-28T14:33:51.141Z","ordinal":460104,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a182ee-8125-7c00-9ecf-eb38ee5a7275","call_id":"call_0c836d0eb5d54fc08df5461b3ba18a07","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 2.9 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 5798)\nTotal output lines: 229\n\n---\nname: using-entire\ndescription: >\n  Use as the FIRST action on any research, investigation, or\n  codebase-understanding request — \"research X\", \"investigate X\", \"look into\n  X\", \"dig into X\", \"search X\", \"explore X\", \"understand X\", \"why is this like\n  this\" — before Glob, Grep, or Read. Recorded intent lives in Entire\n  checkpoints on the server, not in local files, so an empty or unfamiliar\n  working copy is not a reason to skip it. Routes to the right Entire sub-skill\n  (search, recall, explain, what-happened, replay, teach, review,\n  session-handoff).\n---\n\n# Using Entire\n\nYour default when exploring or understanding code should be: **read the\nrecorded intent, don't guess.**\n\nEntire checkpoints capture the prompts, transcripts, and decisions behind every\nagent-driven change. When you need to understand why code exists or how a module\nevolved, look up the checkpoint history first. Only fall back to inference from\ncode structure when no history is available — and label that explicitly.\n\n## When This Skill Activates\n\nUse this skill whenever the task involves:\n\n- A request phrased as \"research X\", \"investigate X\", \"look into X\", \"dig into\n  X\", or \"find out how X works\"\n- Exploring or understanding an unfamiliar module or file\n- Preparing to refactor, extend, or debug code you didn't write\n- Answering \"why is this like this?\" or \"what was the intent?\"\n- Doing pre-work research before making changes\n- Any codebase exploration where historical context would help\n\nFor a research- or investigate-shaped request, the very first action is an\n`entire search` via the `search` skill. Do not open source files or grep first;\nthe recorded history says what was tried and why, and code reads come after to\nconfirm it.\n\nDo **not** use this skill for simple, well-understood edits where you already\nhave full context (e.g. \"add a comment to line 5\").\n\n## First Step: Check Repository Status\n\nBefore diving into specific lookups, check whether Entire is available and\nthe repository has checkpoint history:\n\n```bash\nentire status\n```\n\nThis tells you whether Entire is enabled, which git hooks are installed, whether\nthe metadata branch is initialized, and any active sessions on the current\nbranch.\n\n## Scenario Routing\n\nWhen this skill activates, determine which sub-skill best fits the user's need:\n\n| Scenario | Typical user expressions | Delegate to |\n|----------|------------------------|-------------|\n| Code block provenance | \"why is this like this\" / \"wtf is going on\" / \"what happened at src/auth.ts:42\" / \"tell me why this changed\" | `what-happened` |\n| Research or investigate a topic | \"research X\" / \"investigate X\" / \"look into X\" / \"dig into X\" / \"what do we know about X\" | `search` first, then this skill's exploration flow if needed |\n| Find prior work | \"has anyone done X before\" / \"search past work for rate limiting\" / \"find the previous implementation\" | `search` |\n| Recall a task playbook | \"have we done this before\" / \"recall how we did X\" / \"how did we do this last time\" | `recall` |\n| Replay feature history | \"replay <feature>\" / \"walk me through how X was built\" / \"show me the journey of Y\" | `replay` |\n| Teach a repo topic | \"teach me <topic>\" / \"how does <topic> work in this repo\" / \"give me a lesson on X\" | `teach` |\n| Understand original intent | \"explain this function\" / \"explain parseConfig\" / \"what was the intent behind src/auth.ts\" | `explain` |\n| Generate a dispatch / summary | \"summarize recent work\" / \"generate a dispatch\" / \"what was accomplished this week\" | run `entire dispatch` directly |\n| Code review with intent context | \"review these changes\" / \"review this branch\" / \"audit before merging\" | `review` |\n| Hand off to another agent | \"hand off this session\" / \"continue in another agent\" / \"pick up the codex session\" | `session-handoff` |\n| Convert session to skill | \"turn this into a skill\" / \"make a skill from this session\" | `session-to-skill` |\n| Link session to other repos | \"link session to other repos\" / \"attach session to the foo repo\" | `session-crosslink` |\n| General exploration | \"understand this module\" / \"explore how auth works\" / \"help me understand the codebase\" | _(self — see below)_ |\n\nIf the scenario clearly maps to a sub-skill, delegate entirely to that skill's\nworkflow. Do not duplicate their steps here.\n\n## General Exploration Flow\n\nWhen no specific sub-skill fits — e.g. the user asks to \"understand this\nmodule\" or \"explore how auth works\" — use this flow:\n\n1. **Identify key files** in the target area (entry points, main types, core\n   functions).\n\n2. **Check for checkpoint coverage** on each key file:\n\n```bash\ngit log --format='%H %s' -5 -- <file>\n```\n\nLook for `Entire-Checkpoint:` trailers in the commit bodies:\n\n```bash\ngit log --format='%H %b' -5 -- <file> | grep -B1 'Entire-Checkpoint:'\n```\n\n3. **Read intent for covered commits** — extract the checkpoint ID from the\n   trailer, then use JSON output for non-interactive consumption:\n\n```bash\nentire checkpoint explain --checkpoint <checkpoint-id> --json --no-pager\n```\n\nIf you only have a commit hash (not a checkpoint ID), use:\n\n```bash\nentire checkpoint explain --commit <sha> --no-pager\n```\n\n4. **Synthesize**: combine the recorded intent (from checkpoints) with the\n   current code structure to build a comprehensive understanding.\n\n5. **Report** your findings, clearly distinguishing:\n   - Facts backed by checkpoint transcripts (label as \"recorded intent\")\n   - Observations inferred from code alone (label as \"inferred from code\")\n\n## AI Agent Usage Notes\n\nWhen running Entire commands from an agent subprocess:\n\n- **Always pass `--json`** for commands that support it and might prompt\n  interactively. `--json` implies non-interactive output.\n- **Always pass `--no-pager`** to prevent pager activation in subprocess\n  contexts.\n- **Always specify `--checkpoint <id>` or `--commit <sha>`** for\n  `entire checkpoint explain` — without a locator, the command falls back to an\n  interactive picker.\n\n## When No Checkpoints Exist\n\nIf the target code predates Entire or has no checkpoint coverage:\n\n1. Say explicitly: \"No Entire checkpoint history is available for this code.\"\n2. Proceed with standard code understanding (read source, check git log, trace\n   call sites).\n3. Label all explanations as \"inferred from code — not backed by recorded\n   intent.\"\n4. If the user wants deeper history, suggest `search` with related keywords —\n   there may be checkpoints on related work even if the specific file isn't\n   directly covered.\n\n## Transparency Principle\n\nAlways make it clear whether your answer comes from:\n\n- **Checkpoint-backed history** — you read the actual session transcript and\n  know the developer's stated intent\n- **Code inference** — you analyzed the code structure and are reasoning about\n  probable intent\n\nNever present inferred intent as if it were recorded fact. The core value of\nthis workflow is that distinction.\n\n## References\n\nFor a deeper understanding of Entire's capabilities beyond what the sub-skills\ncover, consult these resources:\n\n- **CLI help**: run `entire help` to see all available commands and flags\n- **Documentation**: https://docs.entire.io/llms.txt — AI-friendly\n  documentation index following the [llms.txt standard](https://llmstxt.org),\n  designed to be consumed directly by LLMs and coding agents\n1:# Task Group: Nexus Code client release and Cloudflare contest publication\n2:scope: Package the installed Nexus Code Linux client and prepare the distinct Nexus Workers/Artifacts contest submission in `/home/mike/nexus-code`; retain separate readiness dispositions and do not treat CI or offline fixtures as a clean-account proof.\n3:applies_to: cwd=/home/mike/nexus-code; reuse_rule=recheck the exact release revision, downloadable artifact, endpoint configuration, contest rules, Cloudflare account, broker installation, and credentials; this records a 2026-10-23 partial result, not portable release or contest readiness.\n9:- rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md (cwd=/home/mike/nexus-code, rollout_path=/home/mike/.codex/sessions/2026/10/13/rollout-2026-10-13T16-47-30-01a13629-7b99-7b61-a6e3-88bb4ac1dd15.jsonl, updated_at=2026-10-23T04:47:40+00:00, thread_id=01a13629-7b99-7b61-a6e3-88bb4ac1dd15, contest CI/video/submission material verified; clean-account reproducibility blocked)\n15:## Task 2: Package Nexus Code as a complete Linux client with installer and CI, partial\n19:- rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md (cwd=/home/mike/nexus-code, rollout_path=/home/mike/.codex/sessions/2026/10/13/rollout-2026-10-13T16-47-30-01a13629-7b99-7b61-a6e3-88bb4ac1dd15.jsonl, updated_at=2026-10-23T04:47:40+00:00, thread_id=01a13629-7b99-7b61-a6e3-88bb4ac1dd15, prerelease/installer evidence exists; representative inference remains unverified)\n27:- for Cloudflare contest work, the user required using the existing repository, preserving unrelated history/work, and distinguishing the Nexus Code client from the Cloudflare Workers/Artifacts submission -> do not replace repositories or wholesale-integrate unaccepted candidates. [Task 1]\n30:- the user required exact downloadable-artifact and clean-environment verification before declaring readiness -> packaging structure, a release workflow, or a fresh-home install alone is insufficient. [Task 2]\n34:- Contest-specific CI passed at `ce7e58b9ce3f6e4a72f84d205c5319b241ca0aa9` in workflow `38471712649`; it covered demo tests and Worker build/typecheck, not a real Cloudflare account run. The service is the submission; Nexus Code is its client/demo harness. [Task 1]\n37:- The stated support envelope is Linux x86-64/glibc with Node.js 20+; do not claim portability beyond it. The rollout records prerelease `v0.1.0-alpha.1`, release workflow `38459471967`, checksum `39548ab8b6ba4043e930f8a30d99ce6f9f787f68c8c46b1b10a9a2f1ea7e5ec8`, and fresh-home installer verification. [Task 2]\n45:# Task Group: Nexus …798 tokens truncated…py artifacts first, then classify tests. [Task 1]\n192:# Task Group: Nexus architecture reconnaissance and context-engineering baseline\n193:scope: Strictly read-only mapping of Nexus information/context flow, durable state, admission, and validation; use when the requested deliverable is an architecture investigation rather than remediation or design work.\n196:## Task 1: Capture baseline for the intended A-I Nexus architecture reconnaissance, incomplete\n204:- Nexus, read-only, architecture reconnaissance, context-engineering, CURRENT-NEXUS-CONTEXT-ARCHITECTURE.md, 2860228, linear transcript resend, context admission, deterministic compaction, durable session state, no context router\n208:- for Nexus reconnaissance, the user required “read-only” and “do not modify source, tests, configuration, documentation, dependencies, Git state” -> preserve scope, record exact snapshot SHA plus concurrent-change risk, and do not implement optimization or prove a preferred theory. [Task 1]\n220:# Task Group: Nexus live Ratatui TUI review and redaction/observer isolation\n222:applies_to: cwd=/home/mike/nexus-code (rollout metadata recorded cwd=/home/mike/Projects/aihost); reuse_rule=inspect the current HEAD and preserve any dirty working tree before reviewing; re-run TypeScript, Rust, and PTY coverage before treating this code-review result as release qualification.\n240:- At reviewed HEAD `2860228`, Nexus had moved from a snapshot viewer to a live Ratatui client: multiline composer, approval overlays, slash commands, cancellation, tool-detail/evidence panels, receipt panels, and default TUI launch in a terminal. `--repl` remains the readline fallback. [Task 1]\n241:- Live launch path: `harness.sh` -> `core/target/release/nexus-tui --live` -> TypeScript `cli/dist/sidecar.js` over NDJSON. The evidence protocol has sidecar-generated evidence IDs, `tool.detail`, and receipt requests; `cli/src/evidence.ts` maintains a bounded redacted ledger. [Task 1]\n251:# Task Group: Nexus CRED-003 FlightRecorder and audit-log credential sanitization\n283:# Task Group: Nexus Cloudflare Artifacts capability, agent-native collaboration, and contest evidence\n327:## Task 5: Prepare the Nexus Workers/Artifacts contest submission, partial\n331:- rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md (cwd=/home/mike/nexus-code, rollout_path=/home/mike/.codex/sessions/2026/10/13/rollout-2026-10-13T16-47-30-01a13629-7b99-7b61-a6e3-88bb4ac1dd15.jsonl, updated_at=2026-10-23T04:47:40+00:00, thread_id=01a13629-7b99-7b61-a6e3-88bb4ac1dd15, contest CI/video/submission material verified; clean-account reproducibility blocked)\n342:- for contest preparation, the user required the existing target repository and unrelated work/history to be preserved; distinguish Nexus Code (client/harness) from Nexus (the submitted service), and never present offline fixtures as real Cloudflare evidence. [Task 5]\n349:- Artifacts should own Git persistence, refs, objects, transport, isolation, forks, and repository token handling; Nexus/collaboration logic should own actor/task authority, intent, semantic contention, validation freshness, acceptance, and provenance. Textual overlap is not duplicate intent, clean merge is not semantic correctness, and ancestry is not authorization or acceptance. Prefer experiments with versioned work statements, exact candidate/evidence links, source/confidence/freshness relevance signals, state-change notifications, and explicit dispositions; avoid all-to-all broadcast reasoning. [Task 2]\n352:- Proposed minimum product: a Worker coordinates two isolated external Nexus runners, uses real Artifacts repositories/forks from one baseline, records exact-SHA validation/review, captures operator disposition, and integrates only the accepted candidate. Durable Objects fit serialized per-run coordination; external Nexus processes should perform shell/Git work rather than running the local agent in Workers. First implementation proof: create/import repository, issue a short-lived scoped token, clone/push a commit, then read that exact commit back. [Task 4]\n366:# Task Group: Nexus Phase 2 independent requirements and architecture review\n370:## Task 1: Independently review Nexus Phase 2 requirements, accepted with recorded concerns for Phase 2S\n378:- Nexus, issue-12, Phase-2, Phase-2S, ACCEPT_WITH_RECORDED_CONCERNS_FOR_PHASE_2S, CAP, AUTH, CTX, DATA, UI, EXEC-001, capability-identity, full-access, context-admission, sensitive-data, no-remote\n526:- security/t5820-credential-remediation-sync, 26b6f7393c317a3578d36da7888c8fb03addb136, 198 passed, 49 failed, 959 passed, Gitleaks, make quality, t5820-release-integrity, t5820-v128-terminal-qualification, git status, explicit worktree paths\n590:applies_to: cwd=/home/mike/Projects/aihost; reuse_rule=revalidate service/release/config state and treat all credentials, sessions, fixture paths, and checkout state as time-specific; never use these notes to bypass authentication, expose secrets, directly reach workers, or mutate protected production services.\n677:applies_to: cwd=/home/mike/Projects/aihost; reuse_rule=recheck authoritative contracts, acceptance records, release identities, and workload/horizon coverage before any readiness declaration; do not infer readiness from deployment health or adjacent phases.\n691:- No canonical `AUTONOMOUS_ENGINEERING_READY` contract or composite acceptance route was found. The verified B+ release, healthy gateway/workers, rollback release, and 65/65 Phase 14 artifacts do not establish AES readiness. [Task 1]\n699:scope: `/home/mike/Projects/aihost` host onboarding, Ubuntu Noble/Resolute platform-baseline work, and the approved commissioning sequence for Dell Precision 5820 `ai-5820-01`; baseline implementation is partial and sandbox review remains a release-blocking boundary.\n712:## Task 2: Implement release-aware Noble/Resolute baseline with pre-mutation gates, partial\n733:- The raw bootstrap gate must run before `apt-get update` and accept exactly `ubuntu:24.04:noble` or `ubuntu:26.04:resolute`. Since `ansible.cfg` sets `inject_facts_as_vars = false`, guarded playbooks must use `ansible_facts.distribution`, `ansible_facts.distribution_version`, and `ansible_facts.distribution_release`. [Task 2]\n734:- Release-aware tests must actually render and inspect APT sources. The Noble and Resolute harnesses were digest-pinned, source-rendering-aware, release-pin-aware, and reported `changed=0` on initial and transition idempotency reruns before the final safety finding. [Task 2]\n752:- rollout_summaries/2026-10-16T01-45-07-stRS-t5820_assessment_and_nexus_v1_release_monitoring.md (cwd=/home/mike/Projects/aihost, rollout_path=/home/mike/.codex/sessions/2026/10/16/rollout-2026-10-16T01-45-07-01a14262-6554-7912-9e3b-5f24d58b12c0.jsonl, updated_at=2026-10-18T01:41:41+00:00, thread_id=01a14262-6554-7912-9e3b-5f24d58b12c0, read-only baseline; decision portfolio and five reports unfinished)\n777:# Task Group: Nexus v1 CI prerequisite integration and release qualification handoff\n778:scope: Integrate reviewed CI prerequisites and accurately track Nexus v1 release gates, claims, and held-out boundaries; use for `v1/integration` readiness work, not as a substitute for exact-RC qualification.\n779:applies_to: cwd=/home/mike/nexus-code (worktree used: /home/mike/nexus-wt/p10g-rebased); reuse_rule=recheck integration HEAD, claim ownership/staleness, exact CI, acceptance documents, and release state; recorded alpha.2 status and blockers are time-specific.\n781:## Task 1: Monitor exact-head CI/reviews and merge PR #10 without calling it a release, success for integration; partial for qualification\n785:- rollout_summaries/2026-10-16T01-45-07-stRS-t5820_assessment_and_nexus_v1_release_monitoring.md (cwd=/home/mike/Projects/aihost, rollout_path=/home/mike/.codex/sessions/2026/10/16/rollout-2026-10-16T01-45-07-01a14262-6554-7912-9e3b-5f24d58b12c0.jsonl, updated_at=2026-10-18T01:41:41+00:00, thread_id=01a14262-6554-7912-9e3b-5f24d58b12c0, PR #10 integrated; qualification blocked)\n789:- Nexus v1, PR #10, bdc705e593def11ee5e44a6ebd0703b57e5953a0, CI 39085388798, v1/integration, 2c70d39ad4f9c29cff04c909eef9de6d2a29bdb2, check_requirements.mjs --enforce, REQ-QUAL-101, ACCEPTANCE.md, CONCURRENCY.md\n795:- rollout_summaries/2026-10-16T01-45-07-stRS-t5820_assessment_and_nexus_v1_release_monitoring.md (cwd=/home/mike/Projects/aihost, rollout_path=/home/mike/.codex/sessions/2026/10/16/rollout-2026-10-16T01-45-07-01a14262-6554-7912-9e3b-5f24d58b12c0.jsonl, updated_at=2026-10-18T01:41:41+00:00, thread_id=01a14262-6554-7912-9e3b-5f24d58b12c0, held-out payload preserved; readiness files untouched)\n803:- reviewers and the release workflow distinguish source/CI acceptance from RC/product qualification -> never call a green CI run a release. [Task 1]\n804:- when shared readiness/governance files are claimed, coordinate claim release or wait for the documented stale threshold; do not access held-out payloads or edit the artifact meanwhile. [Task 2]\n809:- At handoff the version remained `0.1.0-alpha.2`; there was no RC, release tag, publication, or qualification run. `node scripts/check_requirements.mjs --enforce` failed 12 rows: `REQ-AGT-002`, `REQ-AGT-010`, `REQ-PRV-003`, `REQ-REL-005`, `REQ-AGT-118`, `REQ-AGT-110`, `REQ-PRV-111`, `REQ-REL-114`, `REQ-QUAL-101`, `REQ-QUAL-107`, `REQ-QUAL-108`, `REQ-QUAL-109`. [Task 1]\n812:- The readiness claims were `FILE_docs_v1.0.0_READINESS.md/owner.json` and `PLAN-10_READINESS_T2/owner.json`; at handoff the owner PID was absent but the claim was younger than 24 hours. Resume after release or the documented stale-claim procedure, update against `2c70d39`, and rerun fresh branch CI. [Task 2]\n817:- Symptom: PR #9 readiness evidence is canceled/skipped or stale. Cause: it was based on the old integration head. Fix: acquire/release the readiness claim, refresh against current integration, then run fresh CI; never infer qualification from its prior checks. [Task 1][Task 2]\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a182ec-dd91-76f3-bef9-ba00d38b5f54","create_time":1793198031.141746}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
