{"timestamp":"2026-10-28T16:16:37.766Z","ordinal":463609,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"agent_message","id":"amsg_01a1834c-9986-7023-9121-9e48e6a69e93","author":"/root/review_pr302","recipient":"/root","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Message Type: FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: /root\nSender: /root/review_pr302\nPayload:\n**CHANGES_REQUIRED** — reviewed `c39017746f4d8be74062508021b3539ab61a7007..662d084ade9b7c724cc2268cacc60d1ac7a1a19e`. Diff SHA-256: `61f4e40bc135152ffa003c82bdc31fc15703542b5bd9ba3b67f7b6b1edae1cb2`.\n\n- **P2 — `cli/src/checkpoint.ts:1099`:** The new subdirectory restore preserves sibling index entries, but `checkout-index -f -a` checks out the entire index. A sibling file with staged content and different unstaged edits can therefore have its worktree content overwritten during a restore limited to the subdirectory. The regression at `cli/test/checkpoint_restore.test.ts:739–750` stages the sibling content but leaves the worktree identical to it, so it would not catch this loss. Scope checkout to paths under the workspace prefix and add a case where sibling worktree content differs from its staged content; assert both remain unchanged. No tests were run."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a1834a-d12d-7c43-8f4a-96ee044d0ace","create_time":1793204197.7665532,"content_item_metadata":[{"provenance":{"type":"tool","namespace":"collaboration"}}]}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"user_input_order":22083}}
