q <gitRef> -- .`, run from the workspace with hooks disabled, resets only the matching paths and removes paths that are missing from the checkpoint. Use that instead of keeping a custom index parser, which also drops the `core.fsmonitor=false` flag on its `ls-files` calls.\n01M61JMP021G48EEHD5T0V6SN9\tlow\topen\tcli/src/checkpoint.ts\t217\tThe new \"ambiguous layout\" rules match on file names alone, so they refuse ordinary project directories. A directory with a `config/` subdirectory next to any `HEAD`, `objects` or `refs` entry fails `!bareConfig.isFile()`. A directory with `objects/` and `refs/` but no `config` fails `hasBareRepositoryShape && !bareConfig`. In both cases restore aborts with \"ambiguous bare Git repository layout\". The lookalike test only covers the case where every marker has its Git type. Ask Git whether the parent is a Git directory (`git -C <parent> rev-parse --git-dir` / `--is-bare-repository`) instead of growing name-based guesses.\n01M61D5NDZKZRHHHS1XF8VXBK7\tmedium\topen\tcli/src/checkpoint.ts\t212\tThe new bare-repo check hand-parses `config` with regexes and only rejects a parent when `[core] bare` is true. Git doesn't recognise a Git directory that way: it treats a valid `HEAD`, `objects/` and `refs/` as enough. So a gitdir with `bare = false` or no `bare` key (for example a submodule or worktree gitdir), or one whose `bare` comes from an `[include]`, passes this check, and restore will write into it. The parser also misses forms like `[core]bare=true` on one line. Ask Git instead (`git -C <parent> rev-parse --is-bare-repository` / `--git-dir`, or `git config --file <cfg> --type=bool core.bare`), or validate `HEAD` contents the way Git does, instead of keeping a partial config parser here.\n01M61C3JYAE3D3934D411TXF17\tlow\topen\tcli/src/checkpoint.ts\t193\tSplitting out `assertNoNestedGitRepositoryAncestors` copied its realpath, escape check and ancestor loop into `assertUntrackedDestinationSafe`, so every untracked destination is now