{"timestamp":"2026-10-28T16:44:32.039Z","ordinal":464723,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"agent_message","id":"amsg_01a18366-25a6-7091-b038-1334d67f9cb1","author":"/root/review_pr302","recipient":"/root","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Message Type: FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: /root\nSender: /root/review_pr302\nPayload:\nReviewed exact head `b9a986edeeb156802b72d246b67c0f2425d92d9b`. No tests were run.\n\n| Finding | Disposition | Smallest safe correction or test |\n|---|---|---|\n| Whitespace-trimmed Git paths | **False positive.** Git path lists are split on NUL and paths are not trimmed. The `.trim()` calls apply to Git’s `--show-prefix` and scalar outputs, not listed paths. | No source change indicated. |\n| Stale `MERGE_HEAD` after restore | **Actionable.** Restore resets files/index but does not check or reconcile merge state. An in-progress merge can remain active after restoring an earlier checkpoint, affecting the next commit. | Refuse restore before mutation while merge/sequencer state exists; test a resolved merge with `MERGE_HEAD` present. |\n| Non-UTF-8 Git paths | **Actionable.** Git paths are decoded as UTF-8 in path enumeration (for example, `gitUntrackedPaths` at `checkpoint.ts:276–284` and `gitRefBlobPaths` at `:629–644`). Invalid bytes can be replaced, so later checkout may target the wrong path after the index was changed. | Preserve path bytes end-to-end, or detect non-round-tripping names and refuse before mutation; test a POSIX filename containing invalid UTF-8. |\n| Ignored target directory preflight | **False positive for ignored descendants at a replacement target.** The preflight rejects ignored entries in both its Git-clean traversal and tracked-entry fallback (`checkpoint.ts:600–624`, `:649–700`); regressions cover ignored obstructions (`checkpoint_restore.test.ts:1100–1125`, `:1585–1620`). | No source change indicated for this case. |\n| Executable-mode loss for untracked files | **Actionable.** Snapshot and restore use `writePrivateFile` without a mode option (`checkpoint.ts:401–404`), which writes mode `0600` (`durable_file.ts:120–128`). This drops the executable bit. | Persist the original safe permission bits with the snapshot and restore them; test an untracked executable script. |\n| Rename detection in deletion/addition scans | **Actionable.** `gitRefDeletedPaths` and `trackedPathsAddedSince` use `--diff-filter=D/A` without disabling rename detection (`checkpoint.ts:515–544`). A rename can be reported as `R`, omitting old/new paths and leaving stale worktree files. | Add `--no-renames` to these scans; test restore across a committed rename, including an ignored old path and legacy checkpoint. |\n| Renamed `.gitignore` preflight | **Actionable.** The ignore-rule check examines changed path names and looks for basename `.gitignore` (`checkpoint.ts:606–612`). A rename can report only the destination name and bypass the check. | Disable rename detection for this diff or inspect both sides; test renaming `.gitignore` while a target directory contains an ignored canary. |\n| `ENOTDIR` during restore preflight | **Actionable.** `lstat(..., { throwIfNoEntry: false })` can still throw `ENOTDIR` when an earlier component is a file (`checkpoint.ts:322–325`, also `:600–603`). A valid file/directory transition can fail before restore. | Treat `ENOTDIR` as an absent descendant where the preflight can safely continue; add the inverse shape-transition regression. |\n| Staged ignored additions left after restore | **Actionable, with a preservation tradeoff.** `read-tree` removes post-checkpoint staged entries, while `git clean -fd` preserves ignored files (`checkpoint.ts:1077–1080`). A forced-staged ignored addition can remain on disk but silently become unstaged/untracked. | Refuse before mutation when such an addition is detected, or explicitly define and test this behavior. Avoid `clean -fdx`, which could delete other ignored user data. |\n| `ENOTDIR` during checkpoint capture | **Actionable robustness issue, fail-closed.** `assertGitChangesSafe` probes the worktree path with `lstat` (`checkpoint.ts:265–270`); a file ancestor can cause `ENOTDIR` and abort capture. This does not mutate the workspace, but prevents checkpointing that state. | Handle `ENOTDIR` as no accessible worktree leaf after confirming the indexed blob; test a staged path obstructed by a file ancestor. |"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a18366-2421-7770-adb2-8a0eb3a8a68d","create_time":1793205872.038716,"content_item_metadata":[{"provenance":{"type":"tool","namespace":"collaboration"}}]}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"user_input_order":22112}}
