{"timestamp":"2026-10-28T17:00:55.066Z","ordinal":465577,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"agent_message","id":"amsg_01a18375-2599-7591-91a8-09293ec65994","author":"/root/review_pr302","recipient":"/root","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Message Type: FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: /root\nSender: /root/review_pr302\nPayload:\n**CHANGES_REQUIRED** for the current candidate at base `934ed912b8e187c5c418709e472a6313bb4169a8` plus the uncommitted correction. The combined diff SHA-256 is `244e960201823b0f00619ddd0aea096911d375f71d542f1dd9c3d5b19c35f12a`; the source and test diff hashes match the supplied values.\n\nThe dedicated `GitPathEncodingError` now survives `createCheckpoint`’s wrapper, resolving my prior finding. The other listed fixes are present, including `--no-renames`, ENOTDIR-safe `lstat`, untracked mode restoration, merge-state refusal, and ignored staged-addition refusal.\n\n- **P2 — `cli/src/checkpoint.ts:149`:** `assertNoIgnoredAddedPaths` passes a raw filename as a Git pathspec. A filename beginning with pathspec magic such as `:(top)` can make `check-ignore` inspect a different path, causing a false refusal or bypassing the ignored-addition guard. Pass a literal pathspec (for example, `:(literal)<path>`) and add a regression for a magic-looking filename.\n\nNo tests were run."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a18366-2421-7770-adb2-8a0eb3a8a68d","create_time":1793206855.0655947,"content_item_metadata":[{"provenance":{"type":"tool","namespace":"collaboration"}}]}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"user_input_order":22123}}
