{"timestamp":"2026-10-28T17:02:35.601Z","ordinal":465673,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"agent_message","id":"amsg_01a18376-ae51-7f42-84bc-df665516db81","author":"/root/review_pr302","recipient":"/root","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Message Type: FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: /root\nSender: /root/review_pr302\nPayload:\n**CHANGES_REQUIRED** — the combined diff SHA-256 matches the supplied value: `7b0eb9fea72fa18503b9ac5c8895850e194106ed174fa13234a0c7e6e34299b2`. The source and test hashes also match.\n\nThe new `:(literal)` correctly fixes `assertNoIgnoredAddedPaths`, and its `:(exclude)ignored.bin` regression covers that helper. However, the other `check-ignore` calls still pass raw paths at [checkpoint.ts](/home/mike/nexus-wt/checkpoint-ordinary-child-fix/cli/src/checkpoint.ts:801), [checkpoint.ts](/home/mike/nexus-wt/checkpoint-ordinary-child-fix/cli/src/checkpoint.ts:812), [checkpoint.ts](/home/mike/nexus-wt/checkpoint-ordinary-child-fix/cli/src/checkpoint.ts:847), and [checkpoint.ts](/home/mike/nexus-wt/checkpoint-ordinary-child-fix/cli/src/checkpoint.ts:874). A top-level target path that looks like pathspec magic can therefore be interpreted as a pattern during directory replacement preflight, allowing the checks to misclassify ignored descendants. Use literal pathspecs for those calls too, and cover a magic-looking target directory containing an ignored canary.\n\nThe prior fixes otherwise appear present. No tests were run."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a18366-2421-7770-adb2-8a0eb3a8a68d","create_time":1793206955.6013486,"content_item_metadata":[{"provenance":{"type":"tool","namespace":"collaboration"}}]}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"user_input_order":22126}}
