{"timestamp":"2026-10-28T17:34:34.807Z","ordinal":467149,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"agent_message","id":"amsg_01a18393-f737-74e1-85d8-e8e41834a7a8","author":"/root/review_pr302","recipient":"/root","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Message Type: FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: /root\nSender: /root/review_pr302\nPayload:\n**CHANGES_REQUIRED** — delta `87b560c7e581fa71cce270474ba40766f372fb04..1482d6b315129cd46fac61242c2eb91793ed8fd2`; SHA-256 `addca3d069d0e940a5c42f4777034d2188cbceedd6603ea2613319174fb01a3b`.\n\nPrefixing the path with `./` prevents a leading `:` from being interpreted as pathspec magic, so it addresses the `:(exclude)` case. It does **not** make the full pathname literal: `git check-ignore` parses its arguments as pathspecs, and wildcard characters such as `*`, `?`, and `[]` retain their matching behavior. A filename containing those characters can therefore make the guard check a different path and misclassify an ignored staged addition. Use Git’s `--literal-pathspecs` global option for this invocation, and add a regression with a wildcard-bearing ignored filename. [Git pathspec documentation](https://git-scm.com/docs/git), [Git `check-ignore` implementation](https://github.com/git/git/blob/master/builtin/check-ignore.c).\n\nNo tests were run."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a1838f-4c7d-7b60-a64b-253c51d0a9ff","create_time":1793208874.8070338,"content_item_metadata":[{"provenance":{"type":"tool","namespace":"collaboration"}}]}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"user_input_order":22169}}
